From: aiueo52 <aiueo52@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 09:54:47 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 043/105] qsa graph metadata: page-parallel row-metadata kernel
 (SGLANG_QSA_META_PAGE_PARALLEL)

_qsa_graph_row_metadata_kernel is launched one program per row with
num_warps=1 and then walks the whole max_pages page table in a serial
PAGE_BLOCK-strided loop. At the served config (page 64, context 262144)
max_pages == 4096, i.e. 32 loop trips of a strided gather issued by a single
warp -- so the kernel costs ~10 us warm / ~21 us cold independently of the row
count (trace medians: grid [1,1,1] 10.0 us in the draft loop, [4,1,1] 21.2 us
and [16,1,1] 20.4 us in verify/draft-extend).

It runs once per draft step plus twice per decode step elsewhere: 199 us of a
20.5 ms W16 step and 103 us of an 11.95 ms W4 step.

With SGLANG_QSA_META_PAGE_PARALLEL=1 the page dimension moves to
program_id(1) -- one program per PAGE_BLOCK slice, scalar stores guarded to
program_id(1) == 0. Same values at the same addresses, so it is bit-exact.
Flag off, the constexpr folds the guard away and lowers to the previous IR
(verified by diffing the sm_120 TTIR; the only delta is the loop-invariant
page_limit getting hoisted).

Second flag SGLANG_QSA_META_PAGE_BOUND=1 (also default off, NOT bit-exact on
the buffer) stops the page build at cdiv(seq_len, FULL_PAGE) instead of
rebuilding all 4096 entries -- 8x less work at 32k context. Entries above that
bound are never read by the scoring kernel, but they keep stale values, so it
needs its own A/B.

Test: test/srt/layers/test_qsa_graph_metadata_pages.py (launch-planner cases
run on CPU; the bit-exactness parity and the torch-reference case need CUDA).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
